## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of two classic sorts, practical optimization, and memory trade-offs. A hybrid approach reveals ability to tune algorithms for real data patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting threshold and when it helps
- Stability via merge vs insertion behavior
- Space usage with a single auxiliary buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
function sortBatch(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  const buf = new Array(n);
  function insertionSort(a, left, right) {
    for (let i = left + 1; i <= right; i++) {
      const key = a[i];
      let j = i - 1;
      while (j >= left && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
      }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function merge(left, mid, right) {
    let i = left, j = mid + 1, k = left;
    while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
      if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
      else buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    }
    while (i <= mid) buf[k++] = arr[i++];
    while (j <= right) buf[k++] = arr[j++];
    for (let t = left; t <= right; t++) arr[t] = buf[t];
  }
  function sort(l, r) {
    if (l >= r) return;
    if (r - l + 1 <= 16) {
      insertionSort(arr, l, r);
    } else {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      sort(l, m);
      sort(m + 1, r);
      merge(l, m, r);
    }
  }
  sort(0, n - 1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests the integration of fundamental data structures—arrays, linked lists, hashmaps, and heaps—to solve a streaming scenario with sliding window deduplication and real-time ranking.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window deduplication per (user_id, action) using a hashmap with timestamp tracking
- Per-user score aggregation maintained in a hashmap for O(1) updates
- Global top-K ranking via max-heap with O(log K) insertion/deletion and lazy invalidation
- Efficient expiration handling using linked lists and amortized cleanup strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
class EventProcessor {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.latestByUserAction = new Map(); // (user_id,action) -> timestamp
    this.userTotals = new Map(); // user_id -> total_score
    this.userEvents = new Map(); // user_id -> linked list of (timestamp, score)
    this.maxHeap = new MaxHeap(); // (total_score, user_id)
  }

  addEvent(event) {
    const key = `${event.user_id}:${event.action}`;
    const latest = this.latestByUserAction.get(key);

    if (!latest || event.ts - latest > this.W) {
      // Update dedup map
      this.latestByUserAction.set(key, event.ts);

      // Update user total
      const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(event.user_id) || 0;
      const newTotal = currentTotal + event.score;
      this.userTotals.set(event.user_id, newTotal);

      // Add to user's event list
      if (!this.userEvents.has(event.user_id)) {
        this.userEvents.set(event.user_id, new LinkedList());
      }
      this.userEvents.get(event.user_id).push({ts: event.ts, score: event.score});

      // Update heap
      this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: event.user_id});
    }
  }

  getTopK(k, now) {
    this.expire(now);
    return this.maxHeap.getTopK(k);
  }

  expire(now) {
    for (const [userId, events] of this.userEvents) {
      while (events.length > 0 && now - events.head.ts > this.W) {
        const expired = events.shift();
        const currentTotal = this.userTotals.get(userId);
        const newTotal = currentTotal - expired.score;
        this.userTotals.set(userId, newTotal);

        if (newTotal > 0) {
          this.maxHeap.insert({score: newTotal, user: userId});
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
**Time:** O(1) amortized per event for updates, O(log K) for top-K retrieval
**Space:** O(N) where N is number of active users plus events within window W

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-key streaming stats with independent sliding windows, combining deques, hashtables, and a heap with lazy expirations. It mirrors real services that analyze per-entity signals under time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-key deques for time-bounded data
- Hash map for key -> stats
- Max-heap with lazy deletion to keep top averages up-to-date
- Expiration handling driven by currentTime

## Code Example
```javascript
class PerKeyRollingStats {
  constructor(W) { this.W = W; this.map = new Map(); this.heap = new MaxHeap(); }
  add(key, value, ts) {
    let rec = this.map.get(key) || { dq: [], sum: 0 };
    rec.dq.push([ts, value]);
    rec.sum += value;
    while (rec.dq.length && rec.dq[0][0] < ts - this.W) {
      const [t, v] = rec.dq.shift();
      rec.sum -= v;
    }
    this.map.set(key, rec);
    const avg = rec.sum / rec.dq.length;
    this.heap.push({ key, avg, validAt: ts, len: rec.dq.length });
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      if (!top) break;
      const rec = this.map.get(top.key);
      const avg = rec && rec.dq.length ? rec.sum / rec.dq.length : -Infinity;
      if (top.avg !== avg) continue; // stale entry
      res.push(top.key);
    }
    // optional: reinsert from a temp to preserve heap state
    return res;
  }
}
```

